00:02:17.880 --> 00:02:21.630
And then finally, also, EEPROM
and Flash memory solutions.

00:02:21.630 --> 00:02:24.330
Overall, Microchip, with
the security solutions,

00:02:24.330 --> 00:02:27.210
we've been very active
in the IoT space

00:02:27.210 --> 00:02:33.060
to try and simplify how
we can secure IT devices

00:02:33.060 --> 00:02:35.400
and make that
connectivity secure.

00:02:35.400 --> 00:02:41.740
So let's look at why IoT
security is that important.

00:02:41.740 --> 00:02:44.550
So if you're looking at
what happened recently,

00:02:44.550 --> 00:02:47.550
there is clearly an acceleration
of the number of attacks

00:02:47.550 --> 00:02:51.390
that are happening in
the world, typically

00:02:51.390 --> 00:02:53.860
targeting IoT devices.

00:02:53.860 --> 00:02:58.260
But overall, basically
attacks are becoming

00:02:58.260 --> 00:03:00.210
more and more sophisticated.

00:03:00.210 --> 00:03:03.840
And one common thread
among most of those attacks

00:03:03.840 --> 00:03:07.610
is the fact that it's all
about the key material

00:03:07.610 --> 00:03:11.830
or the [INAUDIBLE] materials
that are in the devices.

00:03:11.830 --> 00:03:13.820
That is typically too
close to the software.

00:03:13.820 --> 00:03:15.590
So the software
ends up leaking out

00:03:15.590 --> 00:03:18.180
some of that critical material.

00:03:18.180 --> 00:03:21.910
So in that context, we worked
very closely with Google

00:03:21.910 --> 00:03:25.010
on trying to find an
easy but very secure

00:03:25.010 --> 00:03:28.500
solution to secure IoT devices.

00:03:31.110 --> 00:03:36.210
So looking at-- so that
part, and basically we

00:03:36.210 --> 00:03:41.520
looking at having basically
a hardware root of trust

00:03:41.520 --> 00:03:43.110
for connected devices.

00:03:43.110 --> 00:03:45.490
And what is-- how
do I root of trust?

00:03:45.490 --> 00:03:47.700
So basically, you need to
have two basic concepts.

00:03:47.700 --> 00:03:49.800
So you need to
have a verifiable,

00:03:49.800 --> 00:03:52.850
trustable, and unique identity
for a connected device.

00:03:52.850 --> 00:03:56.520
But you also need to make
sure that basically allows

00:03:56.520 --> 00:04:01.360
you to verify the device
that is connecting

00:04:01.360 --> 00:04:04.392
to the service is the one that
is supposed to be connecting.

00:04:04.392 --> 00:04:06.100
The second thing is
you need to make sure

00:04:06.100 --> 00:04:08.711
that you can validate
the device firmware

00:04:08.711 --> 00:04:10.710
and make sure that what
is running on the device

00:04:10.710 --> 00:04:12.280
is indeed the genuine firmware.

00:04:14.910 --> 00:04:17.070
So device identity.

00:04:17.070 --> 00:04:20.860
It's essentially a little
bit like a birth certificate

00:04:20.860 --> 00:04:22.120
for each device.

00:04:22.120 --> 00:04:25.240
So it needs to be unique,
verifiable, and trustable.

00:04:25.240 --> 00:04:27.930
So it's based on
cryptographic key pairs.

00:04:27.930 --> 00:04:30.410
So you have one private
key and a public key.

00:04:30.410 --> 00:04:33.140
They are mathematically related.

00:04:33.140 --> 00:04:35.490
And you need to--

00:04:35.490 --> 00:04:38.570
so the whole identity
hinges on the fact

00:04:38.570 --> 00:04:40.260
that the private
key remains private

00:04:40.260 --> 00:04:43.580
during the whole
lifetime of the product.

00:04:43.580 --> 00:04:47.300
So if the private key
is ever disclosed,

00:04:47.300 --> 00:04:49.910
that means someone can abuse
the identity of that device

00:04:49.910 --> 00:04:53.600
and can basically
put the connection

00:04:53.600 --> 00:04:55.490
to the remote service.

00:04:55.490 --> 00:04:59.780
So you need to be able to
verify with the public key

00:04:59.780 --> 00:05:03.230
that this device
certificate is valid,

00:05:03.230 --> 00:05:09.830
and basically, you need to
make sure that, overall,

00:05:09.830 --> 00:05:12.840
the private key is always
private during the whole

00:05:12.840 --> 00:05:14.250
lifetime of the product.

00:05:14.250 --> 00:05:16.970
So the bottom line
is you have to have

00:05:16.970 --> 00:05:19.610
a strong isolation of the
private keys from the users.

00:05:19.610 --> 00:05:22.040
You don't want any of
the private keys of any

00:05:22.040 --> 00:05:24.950
of the devices to
be, at any point

00:05:24.950 --> 00:05:26.840
in time, exposed to
any of the users.

00:05:26.840 --> 00:05:29.690
You want to isolate the
private keys on the software.

00:05:29.690 --> 00:05:32.250
Very often, as
we've seen earlier,

00:05:32.250 --> 00:05:37.710
the attacks are really about
the weakness in the software

00:05:37.710 --> 00:05:41.870
leaking out some of
the private keys.

00:05:41.870 --> 00:05:44.120
So the third point is
you want to make sure

00:05:44.120 --> 00:05:45.920
that those keys
are never exposed

00:05:45.920 --> 00:05:49.670
during the manufacturing phase
of the connecting device.

00:05:49.670 --> 00:05:53.230
So overall, the best
way of ensuring all that

00:05:53.230 --> 00:05:57.267
is to make sure that
any of those keys

00:05:57.267 --> 00:05:58.850
are never in the
microcontrollers that

00:05:58.850 --> 00:06:01.904
are typically not designed for
hosting sensitive material.

00:06:01.904 --> 00:06:03.320
ANTONY PASSEMARD:
The point number

00:06:03.320 --> 00:06:05.990
three, Nicolas, is kind of
interesting, because people

00:06:05.990 --> 00:06:07.760
don't realize that
there is actually

00:06:07.760 --> 00:06:11.390
a risk of exposing keys during
the manufacturing process.

00:06:11.390 --> 00:06:14.330
If you are leaving your
keys to your manufacturer,

00:06:14.330 --> 00:06:17.060
oftentimes in countries
where IT levels may

00:06:17.060 --> 00:06:20.150
be a little different,
there is definitely a risk

00:06:20.150 --> 00:06:22.850
that those manufacturers
would copy those keys

00:06:22.850 --> 00:06:26.330
and create fake devices
or copies of your devices

00:06:26.330 --> 00:06:28.825
that are not legitimate,
and can be used to connect

00:06:28.825 --> 00:06:29.700
to your architecture.

00:06:29.700 --> 00:06:31.375
That's something
people don't realize.

00:06:31.375 --> 00:06:33.541
NICOLAS SHIELI: Absolutely,
especially if you end up

00:06:33.541 --> 00:06:36.627
generating keys or generating
the identity of those devices

00:06:36.627 --> 00:06:37.710
on the manufacturing line.

00:06:37.710 --> 00:06:40.520
So that may become a
very complex problem,

00:06:40.520 --> 00:06:43.310
and you may need to invest
into securing the manufacturing

00:06:43.310 --> 00:06:47.180
line in an environment where
secure manufacturing may not

00:06:47.180 --> 00:06:49.374
be that obvious or
easy to implement.

00:06:49.374 --> 00:06:50.374
ANTONY PASSEMARD: Right.

00:06:53.100 --> 00:06:54.540
NICOLAS SHIELI:
So looking at how

00:06:54.540 --> 00:06:59.430
we can implement a true,
trustable, identity

00:06:59.430 --> 00:07:07.790
in your devices, so we basically
have come up with a product

00:07:07.790 --> 00:07:10.610
line of devices that
basically are implementing

00:07:10.610 --> 00:07:13.220
a few basic features.

00:07:13.220 --> 00:07:15.410
One is to make
sure that whatever

00:07:15.410 --> 00:07:18.340
you store in the device, whether
these are the private keys,

00:07:18.340 --> 00:07:22.040
they are securely stored
in a non-volatile memory.

00:07:22.040 --> 00:07:24.260
So that memory's
typically encrypted.

00:07:24.260 --> 00:07:28.530
And you will have, also,
within a cryptographic hardware

00:07:28.530 --> 00:07:31.850
boundary, so basically a
boundary that prevents someone

00:07:31.850 --> 00:07:34.220
from looking what
is inside the chip,

00:07:34.220 --> 00:07:36.464
you have also the
cryptographic engines.

00:07:36.464 --> 00:07:37.880
So what you want
to do is you want

00:07:37.880 --> 00:07:40.520
to have all those features
inside the cryptographic

00:07:40.520 --> 00:07:41.810
boundary.

00:07:41.810 --> 00:07:43.950
If any of those
features is outside,

00:07:43.950 --> 00:07:47.787
your security is
potentially compromised.

00:07:47.787 --> 00:07:49.370
Because the cryptographic
engine needs

00:07:49.370 --> 00:07:51.560
to be able to have access
to the private keys

00:07:51.560 --> 00:07:54.596
to be able to perform
its computation,

00:07:54.596 --> 00:07:55.970
so you don't want
to have to move

00:07:55.970 --> 00:07:58.730
the private key outside of
the cryptographic boundary

00:07:58.730 --> 00:08:00.550
of the device.

00:08:00.550 --> 00:08:02.210
Similarly, you want
to be able to have

00:08:02.210 --> 00:08:04.310
a high-quality random
number generator.

00:08:04.310 --> 00:08:08.450
Without any of this, you won't
have really true security.

00:08:08.450 --> 00:08:10.700
So for instance, our devices
are capable of generating

00:08:10.700 --> 00:08:13.970
their own keys, and they
are typically the output

00:08:13.970 --> 00:08:15.812
of the random number generator.

00:08:15.812 --> 00:08:18.020
So you need to have a very
high-quality random number

00:08:18.020 --> 00:08:21.020
generator, or if
not, you would end up

00:08:21.020 --> 00:08:24.049
being able to devise
keys quite easily.

00:08:24.049 --> 00:08:26.090
ANTONY PASSEMARD: That's
an important point here.

00:08:26.090 --> 00:08:29.270
You're saying that the random
key generator and the keys

00:08:29.270 --> 00:08:32.322
and all of that is inside
the anti-tampering hardware.

00:08:32.322 --> 00:08:34.280
That means-- that's the
hardware you can't open

00:08:34.280 --> 00:08:35.600
with a screwdriver or anything.

00:08:35.600 --> 00:08:37.880
You cannot access
what's inside of it.

00:08:37.880 --> 00:08:40.789
But the private key is never
burned into that device.

00:08:40.789 --> 00:08:42.750
It is generated by
the device itself.

00:08:42.750 --> 00:08:43.834
It is never outside of it.

00:08:43.834 --> 00:08:44.958
NICOLAS SHIELI: Absolutely.

00:08:44.958 --> 00:08:46.470
So this is a very
important point.

00:08:46.470 --> 00:08:49.100
So the private key
is something that

00:08:49.100 --> 00:08:54.350
never exists outside of that
[INAUDIBLE] hardware boundary.

00:08:54.350 --> 00:08:56.840
So basically, you don't want
to generate your key outside

00:08:56.840 --> 00:08:59.450
and have to transfer
it inside the safe.

00:08:59.450 --> 00:09:02.900
So the best way to
have a good protection

00:09:02.900 --> 00:09:05.964
is to make sure that the secret
is generated within the safe.

00:09:09.600 --> 00:09:11.760
So how is such device used?

00:09:11.760 --> 00:09:17.774
So basically, overall, you have
essentially a microcontroller.

00:09:17.774 --> 00:09:19.440
And on the side of
that microcontroller,

00:09:19.440 --> 00:09:24.210
you would be adding
the crypto device

00:09:24.210 --> 00:09:25.750
that contains the private key.

00:09:25.750 --> 00:09:28.170
So the private key is
generated by the device.

00:09:28.170 --> 00:09:31.860
And the microcontroller would
be generating a challenge that

00:09:31.860 --> 00:09:33.900
is changing from time to time.

00:09:33.900 --> 00:09:36.940
Every time you running
that challenge request,

00:09:36.940 --> 00:09:39.000
it's going to be
a different one.

00:09:39.000 --> 00:09:42.970
And that sends that
to the secure element.

00:09:42.970 --> 00:09:45.330
And then the secure element
uses its private key

00:09:45.330 --> 00:09:50.460
and the crypto engine to perform
the cryptographic function.

00:09:50.460 --> 00:09:52.890
And the output is returned
to the microcontroller.

00:09:52.890 --> 00:09:57.150
And the microcontroller then
has a response from the crypto

00:09:57.150 --> 00:09:58.890
device that is verifiable.

00:09:58.890 --> 00:10:02.310
So essentially,
the microcontroller

00:10:02.310 --> 00:10:04.620
will be able to verify--

00:10:04.620 --> 00:10:06.570
or the microcontroller
or the remote service

00:10:06.570 --> 00:10:08.490
will be able to
verify-- that what

00:10:08.490 --> 00:10:12.090
it's talking to has
knowledge of the private key.

00:10:12.090 --> 00:10:16.820
And the device basically
is like a safe.

00:10:16.820 --> 00:10:19.420
So the private key has been
generated inside the device.

00:10:19.420 --> 00:10:22.870
The device is a secure element
with a cryptographic boundary

00:10:22.870 --> 00:10:29.790
that is very protected with
many, many layers of security

00:10:29.790 --> 00:10:34.890
that basically make the
retrieval of the private key

00:10:34.890 --> 00:10:37.932
extremely challenging,
if not impossible.

00:10:37.932 --> 00:10:39.390
ANTONY PASSEMARD:
And that device--

00:10:39.390 --> 00:10:44.610
the ATECC608A here is a fairly
small device, if I recall.

00:10:44.610 --> 00:10:45.610
NICOLAS SHIELI: Correct.

00:10:45.610 --> 00:10:49.810
So the device is available in
a couple of package options.

00:10:49.810 --> 00:10:55.020
So one is an 18 device, where
only a few pins are used.

00:10:55.020 --> 00:10:57.490
But the smallest
one is basically

00:10:57.490 --> 00:11:00.490
a two-by-two millimeter
[INAUDIBLE] that's [INAUDIBLE]..

00:11:00.490 --> 00:11:02.700
So that's something that
is very difficult to spot

00:11:02.700 --> 00:11:04.180
over any PCP [INAUDIBLE].

00:11:04.180 --> 00:11:06.470
ANTONY PASSEMARD: Great.

00:11:06.470 --> 00:11:09.800
NICOLAS SHIELI:
So the bottom line

00:11:09.800 --> 00:11:12.320
is you want to be able to
have the safe really secure.

00:11:12.320 --> 00:11:12.820
Right?

00:11:12.820 --> 00:11:14.970
So the key is generated
in the device,

00:11:14.970 --> 00:11:17.870
and you want to have
hardware protections that

00:11:17.870 --> 00:11:22.100
prevent someone from being able
to get the key out of the box--

00:11:22.100 --> 00:11:26.080
because again, if I know the
key, I have your identity.

00:11:26.080 --> 00:11:26.580
Right?

00:11:26.580 --> 00:11:29.030
So the whole identity
concept is based on the fact

00:11:29.030 --> 00:11:32.390
that the private key
is private and secret.

00:11:32.390 --> 00:11:34.010
ANTONY PASSEMARD: So you have--

00:11:34.010 --> 00:11:35.510
NICOLAS SHIELI:
[INAUDIBLE] a number

00:11:35.510 --> 00:11:37.850
of defenses that are
implementing in hardware.

00:11:37.850 --> 00:11:42.720
So you can see on this
slide the obvious defenses

00:11:42.720 --> 00:11:45.730
you have between the security
device and then, I would say,

00:11:45.730 --> 00:11:48.806
a standard device that doesn't
have any hardware protection.

00:11:48.806 --> 00:11:50.180
In a standard
device, you will be

00:11:50.180 --> 00:11:53.390
able to see all the logic,
all the memory areas.

00:11:53.390 --> 00:11:56.030
That means you would be
able to quite easily,

00:11:56.030 --> 00:11:57.920
with quite standard
equipment, to be

00:11:57.920 --> 00:12:00.500
able to get to the
memory locations

00:12:00.500 --> 00:12:02.880
and read out the content.

00:12:02.880 --> 00:12:06.730
So the security in the
ECC508 and 608 devices

00:12:06.730 --> 00:12:09.530
have, as you can
see, an active shield

00:12:09.530 --> 00:12:11.460
on top of the device
that basically

00:12:11.460 --> 00:12:14.600
is a first layer of
protection, preventing people

00:12:14.600 --> 00:12:17.570
from being able to easily
access the logic that

00:12:17.570 --> 00:12:19.540
is in the device.

00:12:19.540 --> 00:12:20.660
ANTONY PASSEMARD: Great.

00:12:20.660 --> 00:12:23.640
So now we've seen-- we've talked
a little bit about the hardware

00:12:23.640 --> 00:12:27.570
side of things, and a
very tiny crypto device

00:12:27.570 --> 00:12:30.725
that can generate its own
keys and random key generator,

00:12:30.725 --> 00:12:32.670
et cetera, and store
that very securely,

00:12:32.670 --> 00:12:35.400
and serve the crypto engine.

00:12:35.400 --> 00:12:39.740
Let's look at the cloud design
now, with Google Cloud IoT.

00:12:39.740 --> 00:12:43.310
When we designed our IoT
platform at Google-- and we

00:12:43.310 --> 00:12:45.620
have a lot of experience
building IoT devices.

00:12:45.620 --> 00:12:49.580
All of you probably know the
Google Home and the Glass

00:12:49.580 --> 00:12:54.270
and, you know, some of the Wi-Fi
hardware we have [INAUDIBLE],,

00:12:54.270 --> 00:12:57.860
but there's been a challenge
across every type of IoT

00:12:57.860 --> 00:13:00.772
devices, both in the
industrial and commercial world

00:13:00.772 --> 00:13:01.730
and the consumer world.

00:13:01.730 --> 00:13:02.970
It's always the same.

00:13:02.970 --> 00:13:04.070
It's a security challenge.

00:13:04.070 --> 00:13:06.528
That's what we're addressing
today, on securing the device,

00:13:06.528 --> 00:13:10.530
securing the communication and
the identity of those devices.

00:13:10.530 --> 00:13:12.110
The second problem is scale.

00:13:12.110 --> 00:13:14.630
It's fairly easy to
connect a few dozen

00:13:14.630 --> 00:13:17.452
to a few thousand devices,
a few 100,000 devices, even,

00:13:17.452 --> 00:13:19.910
but when you start talking
about either millions of devices

00:13:19.910 --> 00:13:23.020
or millions of messages per
second to a single endpoint,

00:13:23.020 --> 00:13:25.280
this becomes really
difficult to do.

00:13:25.280 --> 00:13:28.820
And this is what the
experience in scaling at Google

00:13:28.820 --> 00:13:31.352
is very valuable in that area.

00:13:31.352 --> 00:13:32.810
And finally, the
actionable insight

00:13:32.810 --> 00:13:33.920
is very important,
because there's really

00:13:33.920 --> 00:13:35.690
no point in connecting
devices if you're not

00:13:35.690 --> 00:13:37.606
going to get insights
out of that connectivity

00:13:37.606 --> 00:13:39.120
and out of the data.

00:13:39.120 --> 00:13:41.210
And that's really a data
challenge, a big data

00:13:41.210 --> 00:13:43.670
challenge, that Google
is very, very good at.

00:13:43.670 --> 00:13:45.290
We have over seven
products that have

00:13:45.290 --> 00:13:49.230
over a billion active users a
month, so we know our big data.

00:13:49.230 --> 00:13:53.990
You're probably familiar with
Search, and Gmail, and YouTube,

00:13:53.990 --> 00:13:55.760
and the way we serve ads.

00:13:55.760 --> 00:13:58.790
Those kind of challenges
of managing and analyzing

00:13:58.790 --> 00:14:01.400
very large amount of data
is one of the core strengths

00:14:01.400 --> 00:14:02.424
of Google.

00:14:02.424 --> 00:14:04.340
So we looked at that
with those core strengths

00:14:04.340 --> 00:14:09.980
and those challenges, and
built our IoT Platform on that.

00:14:09.980 --> 00:14:12.990
So if we look at the product
that we just announced,

00:14:12.990 --> 00:14:16.485
it went off last May and
then launched in beta

00:14:16.485 --> 00:14:19.970
a new service called
Google Cloud IoT Core.

00:14:19.970 --> 00:14:20.960
Excuse me.

00:14:20.960 --> 00:14:22.870
So Cloud IoT Core
is one new service

00:14:22.870 --> 00:14:26.060
that's part of a suite that
creates our Google Cloud IoT

00:14:26.060 --> 00:14:27.050
Platform.

00:14:27.050 --> 00:14:30.710
It is the cornerstone of the
connectivity and the device

00:14:30.710 --> 00:14:31.430
management.

00:14:31.430 --> 00:14:34.610
So we'll communicate
bidirectionally with devices.

00:14:34.610 --> 00:14:36.620
You'll be able to
define your devices,

00:14:36.620 --> 00:14:40.880
manage them from IoT Core,
but then in terms of Dataflow,

00:14:40.880 --> 00:14:43.626
the data will flow from
IoT Core to Pub/Sub,

00:14:43.626 --> 00:14:46.050
which is a global messaging
service I'll talk about.

00:14:46.050 --> 00:14:49.390
You can trigger Cloud
functions, which are serverless

00:14:49.390 --> 00:14:51.080
processing units.

00:14:51.080 --> 00:14:53.410
You can trigger
Dataflows, which are

00:14:53.410 --> 00:14:54.770
the equivalent of Apache Beam.

00:14:54.770 --> 00:14:57.020
It's the managed version
of the open source Apache

00:14:57.020 --> 00:15:01.080
Beam, which allows you to
do processing over windows

00:15:01.080 --> 00:15:02.570
of times, for example.

00:15:02.570 --> 00:15:05.510
Over the last 20 minutes, look
at all the temperature coming

00:15:05.510 --> 00:15:08.660
from those devices and tell
me what the average is,

00:15:08.660 --> 00:15:11.307
and the bands, and am I out
of bands based on an average?

00:15:11.307 --> 00:15:12.890
Things like that,
you do in Dataflows.

00:15:12.890 --> 00:15:15.560
Very powerful tool,
all managed for you.

00:15:15.560 --> 00:15:17.670
And then the data will
flow into a database server

00:15:17.670 --> 00:15:21.030
like BigQuery, our data
warehousing tool, or BigTable,

00:15:21.030 --> 00:15:22.730
for Node SQL.

00:15:22.730 --> 00:15:25.100
Those data will be used
by our cloud machine

00:15:25.100 --> 00:15:30.110
learning engine to create
models at very high speed, very

00:15:30.110 --> 00:15:31.840
high accuracy, and
then those models

00:15:31.840 --> 00:15:34.860
can be used in Dataflow or
even by your devices themself.

00:15:34.860 --> 00:15:37.047
And we have some visualization
tool and Data Studio

00:15:37.047 --> 00:15:38.630
and Datalab, [INAUDIBLE]
for your data

00:15:38.630 --> 00:15:42.440
sent to manipulate the data and
create a better models overall.

00:15:42.440 --> 00:15:46.460
So that entire suite is really
what is Cloud IoT for Google.

00:15:46.460 --> 00:15:48.590
And all those tools are--

00:15:48.590 --> 00:15:50.050
all of them are
managed services.

00:15:50.050 --> 00:15:52.910
There's really no
operations to do with those.

00:15:52.910 --> 00:15:56.540
So if we look a little bit
more into Cloud IoT Core,

00:15:56.540 --> 00:15:57.970
there's two components to it.

00:15:57.970 --> 00:15:59.600
One is the bridge,
the protocol bridge,

00:15:59.600 --> 00:16:02.900
which support MQTT and
HTTP protocol endpoints.

00:16:02.900 --> 00:16:06.020
We have a planet scale ingestion
and a planet scale consumption,

00:16:06.020 --> 00:16:08.600
and I'll talk a little bit
about that in the next slide.

00:16:08.600 --> 00:16:12.170
That's a very important
aspect of our platform.

00:16:12.170 --> 00:16:14.122
We do automatic load
balancing, autoscaling

00:16:14.122 --> 00:16:15.330
is a managed service for you.

00:16:15.330 --> 00:16:17.600
There's no autoscaling
policy to create.

00:16:17.600 --> 00:16:19.160
There's no cluster size.

00:16:19.160 --> 00:16:24.042
There's no virtual machines, no
load balancers, none of that.

00:16:24.042 --> 00:16:26.000
It's an endpoint that
you have in your project,

00:16:26.000 --> 00:16:28.730
in your Google project,
and we scale it up and down

00:16:28.730 --> 00:16:29.840
to your needs.

00:16:29.840 --> 00:16:33.300
If during a month you send
no data or very few data,

00:16:33.300 --> 00:16:34.970
you're going to
have a bill of zero.

00:16:34.970 --> 00:16:38.374
There's really no upfront
cost to using IoT Core.

00:16:38.374 --> 00:16:40.040
On the other hand is
our Device Manager.

00:16:40.040 --> 00:16:41.660
That's where you
create your devices,

00:16:41.660 --> 00:16:43.076
you create all the
attributes, you

00:16:43.076 --> 00:16:45.524
can create hundreds of
attributes on devices.

00:16:45.524 --> 00:16:46.940
This is where
you're going to have

00:16:46.940 --> 00:16:49.280
your bidirectional
communication with the device,

00:16:49.280 --> 00:16:51.930
with the notion
of a digital twin

00:16:51.930 --> 00:16:55.740
being able to set states and
configuration for the device.

00:16:55.740 --> 00:16:57.440
You'll be able to
have role-level access

00:16:57.440 --> 00:17:00.410
control, which is an extension
of the security of the chip,

00:17:00.410 --> 00:17:02.570
and also being able
to access all those

00:17:02.570 --> 00:17:04.430
features through
APIs, command line,

00:17:04.430 --> 00:17:07.800
and console [? scenery. ?]

00:17:07.800 --> 00:17:09.510
So when I was talking
about global scale,

00:17:09.510 --> 00:17:13.160
this is a very important
point for IoT Core.

00:17:13.160 --> 00:17:14.960
This is actually a
very big differentiator

00:17:14.960 --> 00:17:16.220
from other clouds.

00:17:16.220 --> 00:17:19.520
Google is one of the
only cloud vendor

00:17:19.520 --> 00:17:23.180
to own and operate its
own backend globally.

00:17:23.180 --> 00:17:24.470
We own all the fibers.

00:17:24.470 --> 00:17:26.180
We own the network globally.

00:17:26.180 --> 00:17:28.730
That's how we serve ads
on YouTube that well,

00:17:28.730 --> 00:17:30.450
because we have the network.

00:17:30.450 --> 00:17:32.600
And when you create a
device and you connect it

00:17:32.600 --> 00:17:35.620
to Cloud IoT Core, you
only need to know one URL.

00:17:35.620 --> 00:17:38.480
It's called mqtt.googleapi.com.

00:17:38.480 --> 00:17:41.390
That single URL is
used across the globe

00:17:41.390 --> 00:17:44.180
to load balance traffic to
the closest access points

00:17:44.180 --> 00:17:45.380
that Google has.

00:17:45.380 --> 00:17:49.160
That means that if you have
your IoT core in the US, great.

00:17:49.160 --> 00:17:51.020
But your device is in Australia?

00:17:51.020 --> 00:17:52.940
You don't need to have
IoT Core in Australia.

00:17:52.940 --> 00:17:54.481
The device will
automatically connect

00:17:54.481 --> 00:17:58.070
to the closest pop in Australia
and use the fiber network

00:17:58.070 --> 00:18:01.160
to get to the US to where
the data needs to live.

00:18:01.160 --> 00:18:04.300
That gives us a scale that
nobody can provide you,

00:18:04.300 --> 00:18:07.610
and that gives us the ability
to pre-configure devices

00:18:07.610 --> 00:18:09.170
before you know where they ship.

00:18:09.170 --> 00:18:11.900
You can just say,
mqtt.googleapi.com

00:18:11.900 --> 00:18:14.180
on your device, and it
will connect everywhere.

00:18:14.180 --> 00:18:18.720
We connect over port 443
natively, or 8883 also.

00:18:18.720 --> 00:18:21.260
So we support-- we can go
through firewalls pretty easily

00:18:21.260 --> 00:18:23.290
natively with IoT Core.

00:18:23.290 --> 00:18:25.400
That's a very, very
big differentiator.

00:18:25.400 --> 00:18:29.060
When the data lands in
IoT Core, I told earlier

00:18:29.060 --> 00:18:30.320
that it goes to Pub/Sub.

00:18:30.320 --> 00:18:33.190
Pub/Sub is also a
global product and means

00:18:33.190 --> 00:18:35.810
that you don't have to think
about, oh, was that data posted

00:18:35.810 --> 00:18:37.610
in Australia, or was
it posted in India,

00:18:37.610 --> 00:18:38.692
was it posted in Europe?

00:18:38.692 --> 00:18:40.150
You don't need to
think about that.

00:18:40.150 --> 00:18:43.310
You'll just get the data out
of Pub/Sub to a single topic.

00:18:43.310 --> 00:18:45.650
You get [INAUDIBLE]
several you configure,

00:18:45.650 --> 00:18:47.600
and you'll get all that
data in your backhand

00:18:47.600 --> 00:18:49.310
for your application
to be built on.

00:18:49.310 --> 00:18:52.125
So that scale that
we have, [INAUDIBLE],,

00:18:52.125 --> 00:18:53.000
very similar to this.

00:18:53.000 --> 00:18:54.470
There's really
nothing to configure

00:18:54.470 --> 00:18:57.650
for you, something that's
very differentiating

00:18:57.650 --> 00:19:00.412
from other vendors.

00:19:00.412 --> 00:19:02.120
In terms of authentication,
because we're

00:19:02.120 --> 00:19:03.828
talking about security
and identity here,

00:19:03.828 --> 00:19:05.250
this is an important area.

00:19:05.250 --> 00:19:07.940
So here is how we do
the authentication

00:19:07.940 --> 00:19:10.010
with Google Cloud IoT Core.

00:19:10.010 --> 00:19:12.620
The device will establish a
TLS session with a Google Front

00:19:12.620 --> 00:19:16.130
End, anywhere, one of
those hundred-plus pops.

00:19:16.130 --> 00:19:19.010
And then we'll do
an MQTT connect.

00:19:19.010 --> 00:19:21.630
In that MQTT connect, you have
a username that is ignored.

00:19:21.630 --> 00:19:22.460
We don't use it.

00:19:22.460 --> 00:19:23.900
And you have a password.

00:19:23.900 --> 00:19:28.940
That password is a simple job
token, a very common concept

00:19:28.940 --> 00:19:30.620
for web developers.

00:19:30.620 --> 00:19:33.900
That job token is signed with
the private key of the device.

00:19:33.900 --> 00:19:34.400
All right?

00:19:34.400 --> 00:19:36.320
The private key of the device.

00:19:36.320 --> 00:19:38.240
It is sent as the password.

00:19:38.240 --> 00:19:40.690
There is no mutual auth
in the TLS stack, right?

00:19:40.690 --> 00:19:42.800
It just sent as the password.

00:19:42.800 --> 00:19:44.690
On the receiving
end of IoT Core,

00:19:44.690 --> 00:19:48.080
we take that dot, we validate
with a public key corresponding

00:19:48.080 --> 00:19:50.360
to that device that
we're sending the data,

00:19:50.360 --> 00:19:53.670
we'll validate the signature,
and that mutual authentication

00:19:53.670 --> 00:19:55.340
is established at that point.

00:19:55.340 --> 00:19:57.440
So we don't depend
on the TLS stack

00:19:57.440 --> 00:19:59.390
to do the mutual authentication.

00:19:59.390 --> 00:20:01.160
That's a very important
point, because all

00:20:01.160 --> 00:20:04.460
we need on the device
is the private key.

00:20:04.460 --> 00:20:05.360
That's it.

00:20:05.360 --> 00:20:08.420
No certificate,
no big TLS stack,

00:20:08.420 --> 00:20:12.530
no dependency of the TLS
stack to the crypto hardware

00:20:12.530 --> 00:20:13.664
like the microchip one.

00:20:13.664 --> 00:20:15.830
You don't need to have a
special TLS stack for that.

00:20:15.830 --> 00:20:18.975
If you do an update of your
TLS stack, it will still work.

00:20:18.975 --> 00:20:21.350
You don't have dependency in
your code on that TLS stack.

00:20:21.350 --> 00:20:23.910
That's a very important
differentiator as well.

00:20:23.910 --> 00:20:27.320
It allows to have a much
smaller footprint on the devices

00:20:27.320 --> 00:20:32.150
to establish quickly a secure
communication while keeping

00:20:32.150 --> 00:20:36.386
the mutual authentication via
the signature of the job token.

00:20:36.386 --> 00:20:37.760
So this is a very
important point

00:20:37.760 --> 00:20:41.330
how we have worked on a few
different implementations

00:20:41.330 --> 00:20:45.260
on our side, and we've been able
to have a Google Cloud IoT Core

00:20:45.260 --> 00:20:48.970
authentication running on the
small [INAUDIBLE] product.

00:20:48.970 --> 00:20:51.810
Yeah, that [INAUDIBLE]
thing was very impressive

00:20:51.810 --> 00:20:54.420
because I had never
seen it before.

00:20:54.420 --> 00:20:56.510
Because all the
stack is so small,

00:20:56.510 --> 00:20:58.790
it can actually live
in the Wi-Fi module,

00:20:58.790 --> 00:21:00.760
for example, entirely
in the Wi-Fi module

00:21:00.760 --> 00:21:01.790
with very little memory.

00:21:01.790 --> 00:21:02.330
NICOLAS SHIELI: [INAUDIBLE]

00:21:02.330 --> 00:21:04.580
ANTONY PASSEMARD: So it's
very simple to implement.

00:21:04.580 --> 00:21:05.150
NICOLAS SHIELI: Exactly.

00:21:05.150 --> 00:21:05.960
ANTONY PASSEMARD: And
then next to that,

00:21:05.960 --> 00:21:07.376
you can have an
eight-bit MCU that

00:21:07.376 --> 00:21:09.050
is extremely cheap to read.

00:21:09.050 --> 00:21:11.635
You know, read a temperature
or set a few settings

00:21:11.635 --> 00:21:12.260
in the machine.

00:21:12.260 --> 00:21:14.150
You don't need a
third two-bit MCU.

00:21:14.150 --> 00:21:15.770
You can just do
the eight-bit MCU.

00:21:15.770 --> 00:21:19.530
And that makes the whole
device much cheaper.

00:21:19.530 --> 00:21:21.770
So this is more of a
visual representation

00:21:21.770 --> 00:21:23.150
of that authentication.

00:21:23.150 --> 00:21:26.950
In a typical flow, a provision
or person will create keys.

00:21:26.950 --> 00:21:29.540
A public key goes to IoT
Core and the Device Manager.

00:21:29.540 --> 00:21:31.460
A private key goes
on the device.

00:21:31.460 --> 00:21:33.170
Then the device
generates the job,

00:21:33.170 --> 00:21:37.070
finds it, sends it as part of
the connect as the password,

00:21:37.070 --> 00:21:40.070
IoT Core looks at it,
validates the signature,

00:21:40.070 --> 00:21:41.120
and opens the connection.

00:21:41.120 --> 00:21:42.290
That's typical.

00:21:42.290 --> 00:21:43.700
But you see the flow here.

00:21:43.700 --> 00:21:45.530
The provisioner has
to create the keys,

00:21:45.530 --> 00:21:48.170
and that private key lives
in the provisioner's hand

00:21:48.170 --> 00:21:50.720
and has to be burned
on the device.

00:21:50.720 --> 00:21:54.067
So that's where the partnership
with Microchip comes into play.

00:21:54.067 --> 00:21:56.150
The partnership with
Microchip is much more secure

00:21:56.150 --> 00:22:00.350
because if you buy that
cryptochip from Microchip,

00:22:00.350 --> 00:22:03.860
because the cryptochip creates
its own private key inside

00:22:03.860 --> 00:22:06.912
of it, it will generate
corresponding public key.

00:22:06.912 --> 00:22:08.870
When you pass the secure
element to the device,

00:22:08.870 --> 00:22:10.280
it's already programmed.

00:22:10.280 --> 00:22:11.310
It's not in a file.

00:22:11.310 --> 00:22:13.370
It's not anywhere in
the hands of anybody.

00:22:13.370 --> 00:22:15.020
It is inside the chip.

00:22:15.020 --> 00:22:16.370
That chip is put on the device.

00:22:16.370 --> 00:22:18.470
The public key goes
to the provisioner

00:22:18.470 --> 00:22:21.240
and is sent to the Device
Manager to be registered.

00:22:21.240 --> 00:22:23.580
Then the rest of the
process is very similar.

00:22:23.580 --> 00:22:26.990
The device creates a
job token, the job token

00:22:26.990 --> 00:22:30.770
is signed by the crypto
device, the [INAUDIBLE]..

00:22:30.770 --> 00:22:32.930
Natively, there's no
private key floating around.

00:22:32.930 --> 00:22:36.040
It's done inside the SSc-608.

00:22:36.040 --> 00:22:38.600
And that [INAUDIBLE] is
passed as the password

00:22:38.600 --> 00:22:40.380
for establishing
the communication.

00:22:40.380 --> 00:22:43.220
So using that model,
the key is never,

00:22:43.220 --> 00:22:45.410
ever exposed, neither
to the provisioner,

00:22:45.410 --> 00:22:46.720
neither to the device.

00:22:46.720 --> 00:22:49.130
It's all inside that
very secure element.

00:22:49.130 --> 00:22:50.720
And it makes it very
easy to connect.

00:22:54.330 --> 00:22:56.850
NICOLAS SHIELI: So
in that context,

00:22:56.850 --> 00:22:59.730
many developers of
connecting devices

00:22:59.730 --> 00:23:02.970
may want to have their devices
as part of an ecosystem that

00:23:02.970 --> 00:23:03.840
is managed.

00:23:03.840 --> 00:23:07.580
So the way to do this is to
have a public infrastructure.

00:23:07.580 --> 00:23:15.020
So luckily, Google Cloud IoT
Core has the ability to--

00:23:15.020 --> 00:23:17.790
well, you have the ability
to load your own certificate

00:23:17.790 --> 00:23:21.060
authority into the cloud.

00:23:21.060 --> 00:23:25.010
So we can ensure that the
specific authority that

00:23:25.010 --> 00:23:28.160
is managing, essentially, the
whole ecosystem of devices,

00:23:28.160 --> 00:23:30.470
has done a good job of
protecting the private key that

00:23:30.470 --> 00:23:32.011
comes with the
certificate authority.

00:23:32.011 --> 00:23:33.469
So they are
responsible for having

00:23:33.469 --> 00:23:34.510
that private key private.

00:23:34.510 --> 00:23:35.468
ANTONY PASSEMARD: Yeah.

00:23:35.468 --> 00:23:38.380
And that that
certificate authority key

00:23:38.380 --> 00:23:41.310
is on a per-registry setting.

00:23:41.310 --> 00:23:44.120
So we have-- per project, you
can create several registries.

00:23:44.120 --> 00:23:47.340
So each registry can
have a CA authority

00:23:47.340 --> 00:23:48.830
that's different or similar.

00:23:48.830 --> 00:23:52.110
So you can use the same CA in
different registry if you want.

00:23:52.110 --> 00:23:54.020
That's totally feasible as well.

00:23:54.020 --> 00:23:56.570
NICOLAS SHIELI: So the private
of the CA is at the CA,

00:23:56.570 --> 00:23:58.460
and that's why
you're using a CA is

00:23:58.460 --> 00:24:01.170
because they defending
the service of protecting

00:24:01.170 --> 00:24:02.690
that private key.

00:24:02.690 --> 00:24:05.517
And then you typically
have a [INAUDIBLE] of CA.

00:24:05.517 --> 00:24:07.600
So you have, typically, a
intermediate certificate

00:24:07.600 --> 00:24:11.480
authority that is
below that root CIA.

00:24:11.480 --> 00:24:14.440
So there's a signature
in between the two.

00:24:14.440 --> 00:24:17.690
Thing is, very often what we
see in actual deployments,

00:24:17.690 --> 00:24:20.000
people tend to do this on
the manufacturing line.

00:24:20.000 --> 00:24:24.410
And that's a big problem because
the whole trust in the chain

00:24:24.410 --> 00:24:28.930
depends on this second private
key being private all the time.

00:24:28.930 --> 00:24:31.040
Because if someone has
access to that private key,

00:24:31.040 --> 00:24:34.920
it would be able to sign
certificates of the devices.

00:24:34.920 --> 00:24:35.420
Right?

00:24:35.420 --> 00:24:37.280
And then basically,
the last level

00:24:37.280 --> 00:24:39.657
is that you have the
device certificate,

00:24:39.657 --> 00:24:41.240
and the private key
of the device that

00:24:41.240 --> 00:24:44.480
is in a device in
the secure element.

00:24:44.480 --> 00:24:46.490
And the secure
element, basically,

00:24:46.490 --> 00:24:49.290
is the one that has generated
that last private key.

00:24:49.290 --> 00:24:51.220
So that last private
key is essentially

00:24:51.220 --> 00:24:54.320
secure for the whole duration
of their-- the whole lifetime

00:24:54.320 --> 00:24:55.070
of the product.

00:24:55.070 --> 00:24:56.780
So overall, you see
this chain of trust

00:24:56.780 --> 00:25:00.315
may have a weakness
if the second key,

00:25:00.315 --> 00:25:01.940
the intermediate
certificate authority,

00:25:01.940 --> 00:25:04.647
is not correctly stored
in a secure supply chain.

00:25:04.647 --> 00:25:06.230
ANTONY PASSEMARD:
And that's typically

00:25:06.230 --> 00:25:06.710
in the manufacturing--

00:25:06.710 --> 00:25:09.470
NICOLAS SHIELI: That's typically
what we see very often done.

00:25:09.470 --> 00:25:09.910
ANTONY PASSEMARD: Right.

00:25:09.910 --> 00:25:11.650
NICOLAS SHIELI: Because
that's a shortcut

00:25:11.650 --> 00:25:15.470
that you may do when you're
implementing your manufacturing

00:25:15.470 --> 00:25:17.520
line.

00:25:17.520 --> 00:25:19.680
So at Microchip,
we basically worked

00:25:19.680 --> 00:25:22.050
on trying to offer
a solution that

00:25:22.050 --> 00:25:24.570
is very secure for the
customers without having

00:25:24.570 --> 00:25:27.720
to even think about how to set
up their manufacturing line.

00:25:27.720 --> 00:25:30.370
So that second key, which is
the intermediate certificate

00:25:30.370 --> 00:25:33.310
authority, is
something that we offer

00:25:33.310 --> 00:25:35.470
the service of
hosting the plan's

00:25:35.470 --> 00:25:37.480
intermediate CA private
key and essentially

00:25:37.480 --> 00:25:39.240
signing the individual
devices as part

00:25:39.240 --> 00:25:40.950
of our standard
manufacturing flow.

00:25:40.950 --> 00:25:43.690
So you don't have to invest
into a secure manufacturing

00:25:43.690 --> 00:25:44.680
flow on your site.

00:25:44.680 --> 00:25:46.500
You can buy devices
directly from us

00:25:46.500 --> 00:25:49.940
that are signed by a chain
that is completely trustable.

00:25:49.940 --> 00:25:51.840
ANTONY PASSEMARD:
So the customer

00:25:51.840 --> 00:25:55.050
does not need a PKI at all.

00:25:55.050 --> 00:25:57.990
Microchip provides that
entire infrastructure

00:25:57.990 --> 00:26:00.490
with a secure manufacturing
line that's audited,

00:26:00.490 --> 00:26:03.710
that has all the certifications,
can be audited by customers,

00:26:03.710 --> 00:26:04.830
potentially.

00:26:04.830 --> 00:26:07.530
And that means that when
the customer basically

00:26:07.530 --> 00:26:10.410
just buys that cryptochip,
it's all done for them.

00:26:10.410 --> 00:26:12.630
There's nothing
