00:27:17.010 --> 00:27:18.882
So initially, we
talked about the fact

00:27:18.882 --> 00:27:20.340
that you have to
have two concepts.

00:27:20.340 --> 00:27:25.040
So we spent quite some time
on the unique identity that

00:27:25.040 --> 00:27:27.390
needs to be trustable
and verifiable,

00:27:27.390 --> 00:27:29.170
but there's a second
concept that is

00:27:29.170 --> 00:27:30.530
very important to IoT devices.

00:27:30.530 --> 00:27:34.850
You need to have the
ability to verify

00:27:34.850 --> 00:27:37.190
that the firmware that is
running on the microcontroller

00:27:37.190 --> 00:27:38.330
is valid.

00:27:38.330 --> 00:27:41.840
So you have basically
[INAUDIBLE] to have

00:27:41.840 --> 00:27:46.220
a cryptographic key pair
for the firmware validation,

00:27:46.220 --> 00:27:47.510
one which--

00:27:47.510 --> 00:27:49.540
I mean, again, the
private key that

00:27:49.540 --> 00:27:52.170
will be used to sign
the application image.

00:27:52.170 --> 00:27:53.870
So the application
image that you

00:27:53.870 --> 00:27:58.220
want to run on microcontroller
will first be hashed.

00:27:58.220 --> 00:27:59.750
And that gives you
a firmware digest

00:27:59.750 --> 00:28:05.300
and you will be signing that
digest with the private key

00:28:05.300 --> 00:28:09.380
that is the device
manufacturer private key.

00:28:09.380 --> 00:28:12.090
And the resulting
signature is something

00:28:12.090 --> 00:28:14.197
that is appended to
the application image.

00:28:14.197 --> 00:28:16.530
ANTONY PASSEMARD: So this is
another use of the SSc-608.

00:28:16.530 --> 00:28:16.990
NICOLAS SHIELI: Correct.

00:28:16.990 --> 00:28:18.448
ANTONY PASSEMARD:
The first one was

00:28:18.448 --> 00:28:21.230
to store the keys to
authenticate to Google Cloud

00:28:21.230 --> 00:28:22.190
IoT Core.

00:28:22.190 --> 00:28:24.220
It's just with the same
chip, you can actually

00:28:24.220 --> 00:28:26.850
do your firmware validation.

00:28:26.850 --> 00:28:27.850
NICOLAS SHIELI: Correct.

00:28:27.850 --> 00:28:30.391
So basically, you need to have
those two concepts implemented

00:28:30.391 --> 00:28:34.090
in your IoT device if you want
to have a security device.

00:28:34.090 --> 00:28:38.830
So this is the process
to sign your firmware.

00:28:38.830 --> 00:28:40.900
But what is important
is that at one time,

00:28:40.900 --> 00:28:42.760
you actually validate
the firmware.

00:28:42.760 --> 00:28:45.370
So at one time, you had
your whole microcontroller

00:28:45.370 --> 00:28:47.530
with the application image.

00:28:47.530 --> 00:28:51.180
And you have the secure
element, the ATECC608A,

00:28:51.180 --> 00:28:53.942
on the side that actually
contains the public key

00:28:53.942 --> 00:28:57.010
of the signing private key.

00:28:57.010 --> 00:29:01.000
And the host
microcontroller will

00:29:01.000 --> 00:29:03.540
compute the digest of the
application image, will

00:29:03.540 --> 00:29:07.300
send that digest
to the ATECC608A,

00:29:07.300 --> 00:29:11.330
which will be able to verify
that digest corresponds

00:29:11.330 --> 00:29:14.660
to the signature
with its public key.

00:29:14.660 --> 00:29:16.360
And then we'll
authorize or reject

00:29:16.360 --> 00:29:21.960
the application of
that application image.

00:29:21.960 --> 00:29:23.947
So then the secure
bootloader, which

00:29:23.947 --> 00:29:26.530
is a piece of code that runs on
a microcontroller that handles

00:29:26.530 --> 00:29:31.990
the start of the overall
firmware of the product,

00:29:31.990 --> 00:29:36.040
will, based on the
assistance of the 608A,

00:29:36.040 --> 00:29:39.640
be able to decide whether the
firmware should be running

00:29:39.640 --> 00:29:40.510
or not.

00:29:40.510 --> 00:29:47.640
Now, the important point
is that if the 608A detects

00:29:47.640 --> 00:29:51.060
that the firmware that has
been verified is not valid,

00:29:51.060 --> 00:29:53.490
it will essentially lock down.

00:29:53.490 --> 00:29:56.670
It will forbid the usage
of the private key that is

00:29:56.670 --> 00:29:58.020
used to authenticate to Google.

00:29:58.020 --> 00:30:02.950
So that means if the firmware
that is presented to the 608A

00:30:02.950 --> 00:30:05.970
is not valid, the
device will refuse

00:30:05.970 --> 00:30:07.865
to generate the valid job token.

00:30:07.865 --> 00:30:08.740
ANTONY PASSEMARD: OK.

00:30:08.740 --> 00:30:11.140
NICOLAS SHIELI: So you will
essentially be offline.

00:30:11.140 --> 00:30:13.794
ANTONY PASSEMARD:
So you're not only

00:30:13.794 --> 00:30:15.210
preventing the
image from loading,

00:30:15.210 --> 00:30:17.016
but you're also preventing
the use of that key

00:30:17.016 --> 00:30:18.640
to even connect to
your IoT core, which

00:30:18.640 --> 00:30:20.215
is a second layer of security.

00:30:20.215 --> 00:30:21.048
NICOLAS SHIELI: Yes.

