00:35:17.090 --> 00:35:25.120
So basically, FIPS-- it's not
that FIPS [INAUDIBLE] device,

00:35:25.120 --> 00:35:30.490
but you have a number of
things they can do around FIPS.

00:35:30.490 --> 00:35:32.705
You can have a
CAVP certification

00:35:32.705 --> 00:35:34.330
for your algorithms,
which is something

00:35:34.330 --> 00:35:37.680
that we do for the 608A.

00:35:37.680 --> 00:35:38.510
That's one thing.

00:35:38.510 --> 00:35:41.315
And the second thing is you
want to have a FIPS capable

00:35:41.315 --> 00:35:43.884
of random number generator,
which is also something

00:35:43.884 --> 00:35:45.050
that we have in that device.

00:35:45.050 --> 00:35:50.780
So we have the FIPS-based
random number generator

00:35:50.780 --> 00:35:51.630
that is implemented.

00:35:51.630 --> 00:35:54.970
And we have also the
newer specification

00:35:54.970 --> 00:35:55.980
for the help desk.

00:35:55.980 --> 00:35:58.780
So that means the
device will be verifying

00:35:58.780 --> 00:36:02.160
that the randomness of the
number that is being generated

00:36:02.160 --> 00:36:05.370
is at the right level at
the time of generation.

00:36:05.370 --> 00:36:08.190
Because you could have
a very good entropy

00:36:08.190 --> 00:36:10.101
for your random number
generator in your lab,

00:36:10.101 --> 00:36:11.850
but that doesn't say
anything about what's

00:36:11.850 --> 00:36:13.433
going to happen in
the device once you

00:36:13.433 --> 00:36:16.050
are in the field, different
temperature, different voltage.

00:36:16.050 --> 00:36:19.290
So these are things that are
addressed by the help desk that

00:36:19.290 --> 00:36:21.160
is implemented in the device.

00:36:21.160 --> 00:36:24.780
So this is a new stand from
FIPS that essentially tells you

00:36:24.780 --> 00:36:26.430
how to do the random
number generator,

00:36:26.430 --> 00:36:28.709
and we have it coming
back in the 608A.

00:36:28.709 --> 00:36:30.000
ANTONY PASSEMARD: Great, great.

00:36:30.000 --> 00:36:33.000
NICOLAS SHIELI: Also,
in addition to this,

00:36:33.000 --> 00:36:36.070
we have been working
on the penetration

00:36:36.070 --> 00:36:39.050
testing with third-party labs.

00:36:39.050 --> 00:36:42.300
That gives you, essentially,
a [INAUDIBLE] level

00:36:42.300 --> 00:36:45.600
so that you can get an
evaluation on how robust you

00:36:45.600 --> 00:36:48.720
are against known attacks.

00:36:48.720 --> 00:36:52.320
And we've been working
with a lab in Europe

00:36:52.320 --> 00:36:56.610
that is really typically works
on computer certification.

00:36:56.610 --> 00:36:58.292
And the [INAUDIBLE]
level evaluation

00:36:58.292 --> 00:37:00.500
is something that is typically
one of the first steps

00:37:00.500 --> 00:37:02.590
of the computer certification.

00:37:02.590 --> 00:37:06.560
So the combination of the FIPS
compliancy for the algorithm,

00:37:06.560 --> 00:37:08.970
the random number
generator, and the proof

00:37:08.970 --> 00:37:12.790
that the penetration
resistance is very high,

00:37:12.790 --> 00:37:16.590
is something that we can provide
to the interested customers

00:37:16.590 --> 00:37:19.290
if they really want
to look into this.

00:37:19.290 --> 00:37:20.460
ANTONY PASSEMARD: Great.

00:37:20.460 --> 00:37:24.220
So the second
question is around are

00:37:24.220 --> 00:37:26.550
